# LT2's site block for a server whose own Caddy holds ports 80 and 443 (design 9.2, compose.shared-proxy.yaml).
# Append it to that Caddy's Caddyfile with lt2.example.org replaced by the site's hostname (LT2_SITE_ADDRESS), then
# validate and reload that Caddy (README.md). Certificates are that Caddy's business.
#
# It keeps what deploy/Caddyfile does: compression, the 1 MB request limit and no Server header; Caddy's proxy sends
# the scheme (X-Forwarded-Proto) and the client's address (X-Forwarded-For, any incoming value replaced) by default.
# It adds X-Robots-Tag, since a trial with invented data must not be indexed (drop those lines for production).
#
# Logs: no `log` directive, so no access log for the site. Not covered: a request that Caddy itself fails with a 5xx
# (web stopped or restarting: 502) goes to that Caddy's error log with the client's address, user agent and full
# address, an invitation code in it included. Only a global log filter drops those fields (as deploy/Caddyfile does
# for LT2's own Caddy); a handle_errors here would hide them too, but error routes apply to every site of that server
# and the other sites' errors would then no longer be logged.
lt2.example.org {
	encode zstd gzip

	request_body {
		max_size 1MB
	}

	header {
		-Server
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"
	}

	reverse_proxy lt2-web:8080
}

# Optional, for a trial whose emails Mailpit catches (design 9.2, "Reading the caught emails"): the Mailpit viewer on
# its own hostname, never a path of the site, since it shows the emails' HTML. Replace mail.example.org. Mailpit itself
# asks for the name and password (compose.shared-proxy.yaml), so this block adds no protection of its own and none is
# lost if it is changed. HTTPS only (Caddy redirects plain HTTP), no access log, not indexed, no Server header. A 5xx
# that Caddy makes here is logged as above; Caddy writes the Authorization header there as REDACTED.
mail.example.org {
	header {
		-Server
		X-Robots-Tag "noindex, nofollow"
	}

	reverse_proxy lt2-mailpit:8025
}
